.TH SPRITETEXT 1
.SH NAME
spritetext \- read a sprite as text
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B temple/spritetext
[
.B -t
[
.I file
]
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I SpriteText
is a port of the TempleOS
.B Demo/Graphics/SpriteText.HC
demonstration.
It runs under
.IR wm (1).
.PP
A sprite is a queue of drawing elements, and this walks one twice: first
printing a one-line summary of every element, then listing the whole
thing as the code that would rebuild it.
.PP
The walk is
.IR vsprite (2)'s,
not one of this program's own.
A demo whose subject is what a sprite element is should not hold a second
opinion of the vocabulary, and the one it used to hold was smaller: it
knew fifteen of the thirty kinds and printed the rest as their raw line,
so a
.B polypt
summarised itself as five hundred bytes of hex.
It also could not tell a malformed element from a well formed one,
having never parsed either.
The code listing is
.IR vsprite (2)'s
.B elemline
of each element, which makes it what
.I Sprite2Code
emits \(em the source that would rebuild this sprite \(em rather than an
echo of the file.
.PP
.B -t
prints what the window would show and exits, on a
.I file
of your choosing or on the demo's own sprite.
That is how the change above was checked: of the 39 lines, the code
listing is identical and two summaries differ, both of them the kinds
the old walk had no case for.
.PP
The controls are:
.TF "any key"
.TP
.B up / down
Scroll
.TP
.B "any other key"
Exit
.PD
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/temple/spritetext.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR temple (1),
.IR spriteedit (1),
.IR vsprite (2),
.IR wm (1)
.SH BUGS
.B -t
prints the summaries and the code listing and nothing else, so it says
nothing about the window, the scrolling or the colours the window uses.
